<article class="env-lemma" id="0CYP">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0CYP">Lemma <span data-tag="0CYP">30.26.4</span>.</a> Consider a cartesian diagram of schemes </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ X' \ar[d]_{f'} \ar[r]_{g'} &  X \ar[d]^ f \\  S' \ar[r]^ g &  S }  \]
</div>
  <p> with $f$ locally of finite type. If $Z$ is a closed subset of $X$ proper over $S$, then $(g')^{-1}(Z)$ is a closed subset of $X'$ proper over $S'$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Observe that the statement makes sense as $f'$ is locally of finite type by Morphisms, Lemma <a href="/tag/01T4" data-tag="01T4">29.15.4</a>. Endow $Z$ with the reduced induced closed subscheme structure. Denote $Z' = (g')^{-1}(Z)$ the scheme theoretic inverse image (Schemes, Definition <a href="/tag/01JV" data-tag="01JV">26.17.7</a>). Then $Z' = X' \times _ X Z = (S' \times _ S X) \times _ X Z = S' \times _ S Z$ is proper over $S'$ as a base change of $Z$ over $S$ (Morphisms, Lemma <a href="/tag/01W4" data-tag="01W4">29.42.5</a>). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>